; pong.tal - Pong, one ball, two paddles, on the real clock-free wall of a screen
;
;   ./duxasm -o roms/pong.rom asm/pong.tal
;   ./duxsdl roms/pong.rom        <- the only way to play; the ball needs a mouse
;
; The left paddle is the player and follows the mouse's y. That is the right
; control for this machine rather than the keys: Ctrl/button bit 7 says whether
; a key is down, but Ctrl/key holds the last key pressed until another arrives,
; so a paddle driven from it has to notice changes itself, and a paddle that has
; to notice changes itself is a paddle that will feel wrong. The mouse's y is
; already a paddle position.
;
; The right paddle is the computer. It chases the ball at three pixels a frame
; and stops dead inside two pixels of it, which is slow enough to beat and quick
; enough to look like it is trying.
;
; The ball is three by three and moves three across and two down, so a frame is
; a shallow diagonal and a rally is a good deal longer than it looks. It starts
; at the middle heading right, and every time it touches a paddle it leaves at an
; angle set by *where* on the paddle it landed - off the middle and it carries
; on straight, a couple of pixels off and it leaves shallow, six or more off and
; it leaves steep. That is the whole game: aiming at the edge of a paddle is what
; makes the other one move, and a ball that always leaves at the same angle is
; not worth playing against.
;
; A miss scores a point and serves from the middle toward the player who let it
; through. The score stops at nine, because one digit's worth of room is what
; there is and ten would walk off the end of the segment table.
;
; Zero page: $22 the ball's x, $24 its y, $26 its step in x and $28 its step in
; y (both signed shorts, kept as plain numbers and allowed to go negative), $2a
; the player's paddle top, $2c the computer's, $2e and $30 the two scores as
; bytes, $32 which way the next serve goes; $34 to $40 the arithmetic scratch,
; $42 and $44 a segment's y and x offsets, $46 the mouse's y as a short, $46
; and $48 a sprite's x and y, $4c a paddle's top, $4e its x, $50 its column, $52
; its x and $54 its y, $56 its colour; $58 a row of the ball, $5a a sprite's
; mask; $7a set once @draw has run. $7e and $7f belong to @widen.
;
; The picture on any frame is: the centre line and the score in grey 153, the
; player white, the computer grey 85, and the ball last in white so it lies on
; top of everything. That order matters, because a ball crossing the centre line
; hides a dash and the count changes - which is why tools/pongmodel.py predicts
; every frame from the rules rather than a single number.
INCLUDE "common.tal"
INCLUDE "lib/fontlib.tal"

@MOUSE.ylo   = $95       ; the mouse pair is the other way round: low byte first
@MOUSE.yhi   = $96

@draw
        ; Zero page is zeroed when the machine loads, so $7a being clear is the
        ; first frame, and the ball needs somewhere to start.
        LDZ $7a
        JNZ @go
        JSR2 @init
        LIT $01
        STA $7a
@go
        JSR2 @clear
        JSR2 @read-mouse
        JSR2 @move-player
        JSR2 @move-ai
        JSR2 @move-ball
        JSR2 @check-paddles
        JSR2 @check-miss
        JSR2 @draw-dashes
        JSR2 @draw-score
        JSR2 @draw-paddles
        JSR2 @draw-ball
        JMP2r2

@init
        LIT2 $009e              ; 158, 98: the middle of the screen
        STA2 $22
        LIT2 $0062
        STA2 $24
        LIT2 $0003              ; three across
        STA2 $26
        LIT2 $0002              ; and two down
        STA2 $28
        LIT2 $000d              ; both paddles start a third of the way down
        STA2 $2a
        LIT2 $000d              ; and a second literal, because one cannot feed
        STA2 $2c                ; two stores: STA2 takes its address off the top
        JMP2r2

; The mouse's y as a short, in two byte reads.
;
; DEI2 would be the wrong shape anyway: the mouse pair is low byte first on the
; device, the opposite of every other pair on the page, so a two-byte read hands
; back the halves the wrong way round. And the halves do not simply swap when you
; do it by hand, because a short keeps its *high* byte at the lower address: put
; the low half at the base and the value reads back multiplied by 256. The mouse
; starts at zero, so that is invisible until something moves it - and the first
; eleven frames of this game matched the model exactly with the mouse stuck at
; zero, which is how it survived.
@read-mouse
        DEI $MOUSE.yhi
        STA $46                ; the high half at the base and the low half one
        DEI $MOUSE.ylo          ; along: a short keeps its high byte at the lower
        STA $47                ; address, so the other way round reads the mouse
        JMP2r2                 ; as its value times 256

; The player's paddle: its middle under the pointer, and never off the screen.
;
; The pointer starts at zero, so the subtraction goes under on the very first
; frame. That is not a special case to guard for, it is the same case as any
; other frame where the pointer is high: a sum past 32767 means it wrapped, and
; for a mouse it can only mean negative.
@move-player
        LDA2 $46
        LIT2 $000d              ; 13, half of twenty-six
        SUB2
        STA2 $2a
        LDA2 $2a
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2
        JNZ @mp-top
        LDA2 $2a
        LIT2 $00ae              ; 174: 174 + 25 is the last row on screen
        GTH2
        JNZ @mp-bottom
        JMP2r2
@mp-top
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $2a
        JMP2r2
@mp-bottom
        LIT2 $00ae
        STA2 $2a
        JMP2r2

; The computer's paddle. It chases while the ball is travelling towards it, and
; only moves when it is two or more pixels out: inside that it holds still,
; which is what makes the game winnable rather than a wall.
; Is the ball coming this way?  Not "is it on my half of the screen" - that gives
; one frame of movement per rally and then a parked paddle, and the ball walks
; past every time.  But "is the step positive" is not one comparison either: a
; step of -3 is 65533 as a short, which is larger than zero, so a single GTH2
; against 0 says yes for every ball travelling left and the paddle drifts off
; chasing the ball away from it.  Positive means "above zero *and* below 32768".
@move-ai
        LDA2 $26
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2                    ; wrapped, so the ball is going the other way
        JNZ @ai-idle
        LDA2 $26
        LIT2 $0000
        GTH2
        JNZ @ai-go
@ai-idle
        JMP2r2
@ai-go
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $34                ; the ball's middle
        LDA2 $2c
        LIT2 $000d
        ADD2
        STA2 $36                ; the paddle's middle
        LDA2 $34
        LDA2 $36
        SUB2
        STA2 $38                ; and the difference between them
        LDA2 $38
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2
        JNZ @ai-down
        LDA2 $38
        LIT2 $0002
        LTH2                    ; within the dead zone: nothing to do
        JNZ @ai-done
        LDA2 $2c
        LIT2 $0003
        ADD2
        STA2 $2c
        JMP2 @ai-clamp
@ai-down
        LIT2 $0000
        LDA2 $38
        SUB2                    ; the difference the other way up
        STA2 $38
        LDA2 $38
        LIT2 $0002
        LTH2
        JNZ @ai-done
        LDA2 $2c
        LIT2 $0003
        SUB2                    ; SUB2 is first minus second
        STA2 $2c
        JMP @ai-clamp
@ai-clamp
        LDA2 $2c
        LIT2 $00ae
        GTH2                    ; past the bottom?
        JNZ @ai-clip
        LDA2 $2c
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2                    ; wrapped, so above the top?
        JNZ @ai-raise
@ai-done
        JMP2r2
@ai-clip
        LIT2 $00ae              ; and the clamp has to actually write the bound
        STA2 $2c                ; back. Testing for it and then returning
        JMP2r2                 ; anyway is what let the paddle sit one row off
                                ; the screen and stay there
@ai-raise
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $2c
        JMP2r2

; The ball, one frame.
@move-ball
        LDA2 $22
        LDA2 $26
        ADD2
        STA2 $22
        LDA2 $24
        LDA2 $28
        ADD2
        STA2 $24
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2
        JNZ @mb-top
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $00c5              ; 197: 197 + 2 is the last row on screen
        GTH2
        JNZ @mb-bottom
        JMP2r2
@mb-top
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $24
        JMP2 @mb-turn
@mb-bottom
        LIT2 $00c5
        STA2 $24
@mb-turn
        LIT2 $0000
        LDA2 $28
        SUB2                    ; the other way up, as two shorts
        STA2 $28
        JMP2r2

; A paddle. The ball has to be level with one *and* travelling into it.
;
; "Travelling into it" is two comparisons, not one: a step of -3 is 65533 as a
; short, so a single test against zero says yes for every ball going the other
; way. Positive means above zero and below 32768.
;
; "Level with it" is also two, and each has to come out the right way round.  The
; comparisons take the *first* value pushed as the left-hand side, so each test
; here reads the way round it is meant to and not the way round that reads
; tidily - every level-with test wants "too far away, so give up", and three
; pushes before an ADD2 make it compare the wrong pair entirely.
;
; Each comparison gets its own scratch and its own jump, because the two failures
; mean different things and a shared "no hit" path cannot tell them apart.
@check-paddles
        LDA2 $26
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2                    ; wrapped, so heading left?
        JNZ @cp-left
        JMP @cp-right

@cp-left
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $0003
        ADD2
        STA2 $34                ; the ball's right-hand edge
        LDA2 $34
        LIT2 $0012
        LTH2                    ; short of the paddle
        JNZ @cp-right
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $0015              ; 21 = 18 + 3
        GTH2                    ; past it
        JNZ @cp-right
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $0002
        ADD2
        STA2 $36                ; the ball's bottom row
        LDA2 $2a
        LDA2 $36
        GTH2                    ; the paddle starts below the ball
        JNZ @cp-right
        LDA2 $2a
        LIT2 $0019              ; 25 = 26 - 1
        ADD2
        STA2 $38                ; the paddle's last row
        LDA2 $38
        LDA2 $24
        LTH2                    ; and ends above the ball
        JNZ @cp-right
        LIT2 $0015
        STA2 $22                ; out to the paddle's far side
        LIT2 $0000
        LDA2 $26
        SUB2                    ; and back the other way
        STA2 $26
        LDA2 $2a
        JSR2 @english
        STA2 $28
        JMP2r2

@cp-right
        LDA2 $26
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2                    ; wrapped: not heading this way
        JNZ @cp-nohit
        LDA2 $26
        LIT2 $0000
        GTH2
        JNZ @cp-r-go
        JMP2r2
@cp-r-go
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $0003
        ADD2
        STA2 $34
        LDA2 $34
        LIT2 $012b              ; 299
        LTH2                    ; short of the paddle
        JNZ @cp-nohit
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $012d              ; 301 = 299 + 3 - 1: the ball's own width carries
        GTH2                    ; it past, so 301 is the last touching position
        JNZ @cp-nohit
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $0002
        ADD2
        STA2 $36
        LDA2 $2c
        LDA2 $36
        GTH2
        JNZ @cp-nohit
        LDA2 $2c
        LIT2 $0019
        ADD2
        STA2 $38
        LDA2 $38
        LDA2 $24
        LTH2
        JNZ @cp-nohit
        LIT2 $0128              ; 296 = 299 - 3. $012c is 300, which lets the
        STA2 $22                ; ball sit two pixels inside the paddle
        LIT2 $0000
        LDA2 $26
        SUB2
        STA2 $26
        LDA2 $2c
        JSR2 @english
        STA2 $28
@cp-nohit
        JMP2r2

; A miss, which is the only place the score changes.
@check-miss
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $0003
        ADD2
        LIT2 $0012              ; 18
        LTH2                    ; the whole ball past the left paddle?
        JNZ @cm-left
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $012d              ; 301: $012e and $012f are 302 and 303, and a
        GTH2
        JNZ @cm-right
        JMP2r2
@cm-left
        LDZ $30
        LIT $01
        ADD
        STA $30
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $32                ; next serve goes left, to whoever let it past
        JMP2 @serve
@cm-right
        LDZ $2e
        LIT $01
        ADD
        STA $2e
        LIT2 $0001
        STA2 $32
        JMP2 @serve

@serve
        LIT2 $009e
        STA2 $22
        LIT2 $0062
        STA2 $24
        LDZ2 $32
        JNZ @sv-right
        LIT2 $0000
        LIT2 $0003
        SUB2                    ; -3
        STA2 $26
        JMP @sv-dy
@sv-right
        LIT2 $0003
        STA2 $26
@sv-dy
        LIT2 $0002
        STA2 $28
        JMP2r2

; Stack: the paddle's top. Leaves the ball's new step in y.
;
; Where it lands on the paddle decides where it goes. Straight through the middle
; and it keeps the angle it had - which is the honest answer, because a ball that
; changed direction at the exact centre would be a ball with a mind of its own.
; Two to five pixels off and it leaves shallow, six or more and steep. The three
; thresholds are the whole skill of the game, and they are why the paddle is
; worth aiming at rather than just parking.
@english
        STA2 $3a                ; the paddle's top
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $3c                ; the ball's middle
        LDA2 $3a
        LIT2 $000d
        ADD2
        STA2 $3e                ; the paddle's middle
        LDA2 $3c
        LDA2 $3e
        SUB2                    ; where on the paddle it landed
        STA2 $40
        LDA2 $40
        LIT2 $8000
        GTH2
        JNZ @en-below
        LDA2 $40
        LIT2 $0000
        GTH2                    ; above the middle?  Not EQU2 and JNZ: EQU2
        JNZ @en-above           ; pushes one *on* equality, so that pair jumps
        LDA2 $28                ; away from the dead-centre case, which is the
        JMP2r2                  ; one case that has to carry on as it was
@en-above
        LDA2 $40
        LIT2 $0005
        GTH2
        JNZ @en-up3
        JMP2 @en-up2
@en-below
        LIT2 $0000
        LDA2 $40
        SUB2                    ; the other way up, so it can be compared with 2
        STA2 $40
        LDA2 $40
        LIT2 $0005
        GTH2
        JNZ @en-dn3
        JMP2 @en-dn2
@en-up3
        LIT2 $0003
        JMP2r2
@en-up2
        LIT2 $0002
        JMP2r2
@en-dn3
        LIT2 $fffd              ; -3
        JMP2r2
@en-dn2
        LIT2 $fffe              ; -2
        JMP2r2

; ------------------------------------------------------------------ drawing

; The centre line: thirteen dashes of eight rows, one every sixteen. Written as
; a loop over rows with an "eight on, eight off" test rather than as a 200-byte
; mask, because a mask that tall does not fit a table and this costs about two
; thousand steps.
@draw-dashes
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $34                ; the row
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $36                ; the row within the sixteen of a dash and a gap
@dd-row
        LDA2 $36
        LIT2 $0008
        LTH2                    ; the first eight of the sixteen are the dash
        JNZ @dd-plot
        JMP @dd-next
@dd-plot
        LIT2 $009f              ; 159
        LDA2 $34
        LIT $02                ; the colour, pushed with LIT: LDZ $02 would read
        JSR2 @plot             ; zero-page address 2, which is zero
@dd-next
        LDA2 $36
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $36                ; both counters move on every row. Advancing only
        LDA2 $34                ; the row counter once per sixteen leaves it
        LIT2 $0001              ; sitting still through eight plots, and the
        ADD2                    ; centre line comes out as two hundred solid
        STA2 $34                ; pixels instead of thirteen dashes.
        LDA2 $36
        LIT2 $0010              ; sixteen rows: a dash and a gap, then again
        LTH2
        JNZ @dd-row
        LIT2 $0000
        STA2 $36
        LDA2 $34
        LIT2 $00c8              ; 200
        LTH2
        JNZ @dd-row
        JMP2r2

; The score, one digit each side, at the top, out of the same bitmap font
; clock.rom uses: eight wide, eight down, one byte a row, bit 7 the leftmost
; pixel.
;
; These were seven-segment figures, and a seven-segment figure cannot be a
; right-shaped digit - the 1 has no foot, the 4 is open-topped, the 7 is bare, and
; the shape is not in the data at all, it is in the seven pieces. @num draws the
; font bytes as they are, so the score is the same '1' the clock shows.
;
; @num wants x, y, value and colour; the old @digit took only the first three and
; read its colour from zero page, which is why the digit call here grew a fourth
; line.
@draw-score
        LIT2 $0046              ; 70, the player's score
        LIT2 $0008
        LDZ $2e
        LIT $01                ; white, like the player's paddle
        JSR2 @num
        LIT2 $00f2              ; 242, the computer's
        LIT2 $0008
        LDZ $30
        LIT $03                ; grey 85, like the computer's paddle
        JSR2 @num
        JMP2r2

; Both paddles, three columns of twenty-six. The colour is a byte in $4f, set by
; the caller, because a routine that took it on the stack would have three
; arguments and every one of them the wrong width for something else.
@draw-paddles
        LIT $01
        STA $56
        LIT2 $0012              ; 18
        LDA2 $2a
        JSR2 @paddle
        LIT $03
        STA $56
        LIT2 $012b              ; 299
        LDA2 $2c
        JSR2 @paddle
        JMP2r2

; Stack: the paddle's x, then its top. Twenty-six rows by three columns, drawn a
; pixel at a time because a three-wide column is one sprite and a two-row slice
; of it is not, and 78 pixels is not worth a mask table to save.
@paddle
        STA2 $4c                ; the top
        STA2 $4e                ; the x
        LDA2 $4c
        LIT2 $001a              ; twenty-six rows, so the loop stops at top + 26
        ADD2
        STA2 $60                ; worked out once, before the row loop, because
        LIT2 $0000              ; three pushes and an ADD2 inside the loop is
        STA2 $50                ; how you get a test on the wrong pair
@pd-col
        LDA2 $4e
        LDA2 $50
        ADD2
        STA2 $52
        LDA2 $4c
        STA2 $54
@pd-row
        LDA2 $52
        LDA2 $54
        LDZ $56
        JSR2 @plot
        LDA2 $54
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $54
        LDA2 $54
        LDA2 $60
        LTH2
        JNZ @pd-row
        LDA2 $50
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $50
        LDA2 $50
        LIT2 $0003
        LTH2
        JNZ @pd-col
        JMP2r2

; The ball: three by three at ($22, $24), in white, drawn last so that it lies
; on top of the centre line and of a paddle. A ball crossing a dash hides it,
; and that is a real change in the frame's pixel count, which is why the model
; predicts every frame from the rules rather than one number for all of them.
@draw-ball
        LDA2 $24
        STA2 $58
@bl-row
        LDA2 $22
        LDA2 $58
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @plot
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        LDA2 $58
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @plot
        LDA2 $22
        LIT2 $0002
        ADD2
        LDA2 $58
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @plot
        LDA2 $58
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $58
        LDA2 $24
        LIT2 $0003
        ADD2
        STA2 $34                ; the ball's last row plus one
        LDA2 $58
        LDA2 $34
        LTH2                    ; three rows: this one, and two more
        JNZ @bl-row
        JMP2r2

